## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your understanding of counting DP, covering both memoization (top-down) and tabulation (bottom-up) approaches, along with space optimization and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts

- Subset sum counting with each element used at most once
- Memoization (top-down) with recursive DFS and memo table
- Tabulation (bottom-up) with 1D space optimization to O(S)
- Modulo arithmetic to prevent integer overflow

## Edge Cases

- **Empty subset (N=0)**: If N=0 and S=0, return 1 (one valid subset - empty set). If S>0, return 0.
- **Target S=0**: Always return 1 (the empty subset counts as one valid subset).
- **All elements greater than S**: No valid subsets exist, return 0.

## Code Example

```javascript
function countSubsets(nums, target) {
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const n = nums.length;

  // Top-down with memoization
  const memo = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(target + 1).fill(-1));
  function dfs(i, s) {
    if (s === 0) return 1;
    if (i === 0) return 0;
    if (memo[i][s] !== -1) return memo[i][s];
    memo[i][s] = dfs(i - 1, s) % mod;
    if (s >= nums[i - 1]) {
      memo[i][s] = (memo[i][s] + dfs(i - 1, s - nums[i - 1])) % mod;
    }
    return memo[i][s];
  }
  return dfs(n, target);
}

// Bottom-up with space optimization
function countSubsetsBU(nums, target) {
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const dp = Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;

  for (const num of nums) {
    for (let s = target; s >= num; s--) {
      dp[s] = (dp[s] + dp[s - num]) % mod;
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

Both solutions run in O(N×S) time with the bottom-up approach using O(S) space.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine basic structures (arrays/deque, hashmap, heap) to maintain a sliding window’s frequency distribution and extract top-K on demand.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance with a deque
- Frequency counting via a hashmap
- Min-heap with lazy deletions for top-K
- Amortized update and top-K retrieval costs

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W
    this.window = [] // acts as deque (push is at end, shift from start)
    this.counts = new Map()
    this.heap = [] // min-heap of [freq, value]
  }
  _pushHeap(pair) { /* heap push for [freq, value] */ }
  add(x) {
    this.window.push(x)
    this.counts.set(x, (this.counts.get(x) || 0) + 1)
    // push updated freq
    this._pushHeap([this.counts.get(x), x])
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      const y = this.window.shift()
      const f = (this.counts.get(y) || 0) - 1
      if (f <= 0) this.counts.delete(y)
      else this.counts.set(y, f)
      this._pushHeap([f, y])
    }
  }
  topK(K) {
    const res = []
    const seen = new Set()
    const pulled = []
    while (res.length < Math.min(K, this.counts.size) && this.heap.length) {
      const [freq, val] = this._popHeap()
      if (!this.counts.has(val) || this.counts.get(val) !== freq) continue
      if (!seen.has(val)) {
        seen.add(val)
        res.push(val)
        pulled.push([freq, val])
      }
    }
    // restore elements for future calls
    for (const p of pulled) this._pushHeap(p)
    return res
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests robustness of sorting mindset under memory constraints and heavy duplicates, plus guarantees on worst-case performance.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: quicksort with a depth limit that falls back to heapsort
- Three-way partition: handles many duplicates efficiently
- In-place, cache-friendly data movement and tail recursion optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
function partitionDutch(a, lo, hi, pivot){
  let i = lo, lt = lo, gt = hi;
  while (i <= gt){
    if (a[i] < pivot){ [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (a[i] > pivot){ [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  return [lt, gt];
}
```

