## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about multi-key sorting, stability, and practical trade-offs in beginner-friendly tasks. It also aligns with real-world data processing where groupings matter.

## Key Concepts

- Sorting stability and its impact on multi-key ordering
- Two-pass stable sorting technique
- Time complexity: O(n log n) for mergesort
- Space complexity: O(n) auxiliary memory

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableMergeSortBy(arr, key) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2)
  const left = stableMergeSortBy(arr.slice(0, mid), key)
  const right = stableMergeSortBy(arr.slice(mid), key)
  const out = []
  let i = 0, j = 0
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i][key] <= right[j][key]) out.push(left[i++])
    else out.push(right[j++])
  }
  return out.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j))
}

function sortRecords(arr) {
  const byTime = stableMergeSortBy(arr, 'time')
  return stableMergeSortBy(byTime, 'priority')
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify if you must sort in place and the library sort is not stable?
- If time values span a large range, how could you optimize space or use counting/radix techniques to reduce complexity?

## Why This Is Asked

Duplicates are common in real data and inputs are often skewed. A 3-way partition quicksort directly handles the middle bucket of equal keys, avoiding repeated swaps of equal elements and preventing degeneracy.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag style) for handling duplicates
- Pivot selection strategies (median-of-three to reduce worst-case risk)
- Introsort/hybrid switching to guarantee worst-case bounds
- In-place sorting with O(log n) recursion depth

## Code Example

```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  while (lo < hi) {
    let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;
    const pivot = a[lo];
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (a[i] < pivot) { [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (a[i] > pivot) { [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt; // tail recursion on the right side
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify the pivot strategy to further reduce worst-case risk?
- How would you adapt this to a non-recursive implementation to save stack depth?
- Compare in-place 3-way quicksort with a stable sort like mergesort in terms of cache efficiency and memory usage.
